Notebook[{Cell["Summa", "Title"], 
  Cell["A guided tour: from a trace to tensor networks", "Subtitle"], 
  Cell["0. Welcome", "Section"], Cell["Summa contracts tensors with the \
syntax of numpy.einsum: you write the indices of a product as on paper, and \
Summa finds the cheapest order of the contractions and runs each one on fast \
kernels. This tour starts from a trace and builds up, one piece at a time: \
the syntax, indices shared by any number of tensors, the drawing of the \
network, the order of the contractions and its cost, speed, and a tensor \
network checked by hand.", "Text"], Cell["How to use this notebook: evaluate \
the cells in order (Shift+Enter, or Evaluation > Evaluate Notebook); the \
whole tour takes a few seconds. The comment under each input says what you \
should get. The same file runs as a script: wolfram -script Examples.wl.", 
   "Text"], Cell["Loading the package", "Subsection"], 
  Cell["(* the paclet next to this file (the repository), otherwise the \
installed one *)\nWith[{folder = Quiet[If[$InputFileName =!= \"\", \
DirectoryName[$InputFileName], NotebookDirectory[]]]},\n  \
If[StringQ[folder],\n   Scan[If[FileExistsQ[FileNameJoin[{#, \
\"PacletInfo.wl\"}]], PacletDirectoryLoad[#]] &, {FileNameJoin[{folder, \
\"Summa\"}], folder}]]];\nNeeds[\"Summa`\"];", "Input"], 
  Cell["$SummaVersion\n(* the version of the package, e.g. \"1.0.0\" *)", 
   "Input"], Cell["1. Indices as on paper", "Section"], 
  Cell["An einsum names the indices of every tensor, separated by commas, and \
after the arrow those of the result; an index that does not reach the result \
is summed. The matrix product, the trace, the diagonal and the transpose:", 
   "Text"], Cell["m = {{a, b}, {c, d}};\nEinsum[\"ij,jk->ik\", m, m]\n(* \
{{a^2 + b c, a b + b d}, {a c + c d, b c + d^2}}: the same as m . m *)", 
   "Input"], Cell["{Einsum[\"ii->\", m], Einsum[\"ii->i\", m], \
Einsum[\"ij->ji\", m]}\n(* {a + d, {a, d}, {{a, c}, {b, d}}}: the trace, the \
diagonal, the transpose *)", "Input"], Cell["Without an arrow, the result has \
the indices that appear once, in alphabetical order (as in numpy): \"ij,jk\" \
is the product, \"ba\" a transpose.", "Text"], Cell["{Einsum[\"ij,jk\", m, m] \
== m . m, Einsum[\"ba\", m] == Transpose[m]}\n(* {True, True} *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["2. Batches and broadcasting", "Section"], 
  Cell["An ellipsis stands for any number of leading indices, shared and \
broadcast as in numpy: a batch of products in one call.", "Text"], 
  Cell["SeedRandom[1]; batch = RandomReal[1, {5, 3, \
3}];\nEinsum[\"...ij,...jk->...ik\", batch, batch] == (#.# & /@ batch)\n(* \
True: five products of 3 x 3 matrices *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["Indices can be any characters, Unicode included, and the list syntax \
takes labels of any kind:", "Text"], Cell["{Einsum[\"\\[Alpha]\\[Beta],\\[Bet\
a]\\[Gamma]->\\[Alpha]\\[Gamma]\", m, m] == m . m, Einsum[{{\"left\", \
\"bond\"}, {\"bond\", \"right\"}} -> {\"left\", \"right\"}, m, m] == m . \
m}\n(* {True, True} *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["3. An index in any number of tensors", "Section"], 
  Cell["In numpy and in Summa an index may appear in any number of tensors: \
here i is shared by three vectors.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Einsum[\"i,i,i->\", {a, b}, {c, d}, {e, f}]\n(* a c e + b d f *)", 
   "Input"], Cell["The canonical (CP) decomposition of a tensor of order \
three is rebuilt by one einsum, in which i joins three matrices: a \
hyperedge.", "Text"], Cell["SeedRandom[2]; {u, v, w} = RandomReal[1, {3, 4, \
2}];\nEinsum[\"ai,bi,ci->abc\", u, v, w] == Table[Sum[u[[p, i]] v[[q, i]] \
w[[r, i]], {i, 2}], {p, 4}, {q, 4}, {r, 4}]\n(* True *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["A repeated index inside one tensor takes its diagonal, in any number \
of its levels:", "Text"], Cell["t = Array[x, {2, 2, 2}];\nEinsum[\"iii->i\", \
t]\n(* {x[1, 1, 1], x[2, 2, 2]} *)", "Input"], Cell["4. The network, drawn", 
   "Section"], Cell["EinsumDiagram draws the tensor network of an einsum: the \
tensors are nodes, the contracted indices bonds, the indices of the result \
open legs, and an index shared by three or more tensors a hyperedge (a dot)."\
, "Text"], Cell["EinsumDiagram[\"ai,bi,ci->abc\"]\n(* three tensors with an \
open leg each, joined by the hyperedge i *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["The norm of a matrix-product state of four sites: the state in the \
top row, its conjugate in the bottom one.", "Text"], 
  Cell["mps = \"pa,aqb,brc,cs,pA,AqB,BrC,Cs->\";\nEinsumDiagram[mps]\n(* two \
rows of four tensors: the bonds a, b, c and A, B, C along the rows, the \
physical indices p, q, r, s between them *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["5. The order of the contractions", "Section"], 
  Cell["An einsum of many tensors is done one pair at a time, and the order \
decides the cost. EinsumPath gives the order that Summa chooses, from the \
shapes alone, with its cost in flops, the cost of the naive sum and the \
speed-up:", "Text"], Cell["dims = {{2, 32}, {32, 2, 32}, {32, 2, 32}, {32, \
2}, {2, 32}, {32, 2, 32}, {32, 2, 32}, {32, 2}};\npath = EinsumPath[mps, \
dims];\nLookup[path, {\"Flops\", \"NaiveFlops\", \"Speedup\"}]\n(* {17424., \
1.20259*10^11, 6.90192*10^6}: the naive sum is seven million times slower *)"\
, "Input"], Cell["path[\"Contractions\"]\n(* the pairwise steps, as einsums: \
each half of the network closed site by site, then the two halves joined *)", 
   "Input"], 
  Cell["EinsumTree draws the same order, with the cost of every step:", 
   "Text"], Cell["EinsumTree[mps, dims]\n(* a tree with 4096 flops for the \
first steps and a scalar at the root *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["The order can be chosen: \"Optimal\" (an exact search, up to eight \
tensors by default), \"Greedy\", \"Sequential\" (left to right), or a list of \
pairs.", "Text"], Cell["Lookup[EinsumPath[mps, dims, \"Path\" -> #], \
\"Flops\"] & /@ {\"Optimal\", \"Greedy\", \"Sequential\"}\n(* {17424., \
17424., 25664.}: here the greedy order is optimal, and left to right costs \
half as much again *)", "Input"], Cell["6. Fast kernels", "Section"], 
  Cell["Each pairwise step runs on BLAS Dot, on a batched kernel or on \
broadcasting, after as few transposes as possible. The reconstruction of a 40 \
x 40 x 40 tensor from its CP decomposition, against the same sum written \
out:", "Text"], Cell["SeedRandom[3]; {u, v, w} = RandomReal[1, {3, 40, \
8}];\n{First[RepeatedTiming[Einsum[\"ai,bi,ci->abc\", u, v, w]]], \
First[AbsoluteTiming[Table[Sum[u[[p, i]] v[[q, i]] w[[r, i]], {i, 8}], {p, \
40}, {q, 40}, {r, 40}]]]}\n(* the times in seconds: Einsum one or two orders \
of magnitude faster than the explicit sum *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["A second call with the same shapes reuses the plan from a cache; \
EinsumFunction plans once for fixed shapes, for loops.", "Text"], 
  Cell["SeedRandom[4]; {x1, x2} = RandomReal[1, {2, 1000, 4, 4}];\nproduct = \
EinsumFunction[\"bij,bjk->bik\", {{1000, 4, 4}, {1000, 4, 4}}];\n{product[x1, \
x2] == MapThread[Dot, {x1, x2}], product[\"Flops\"]}\n(* {True, 64000.} *)", 
   "Input"], Cell["7. Exact, symbolic, complex and sparse entries", 
   "Section"], Cell["The entries can be anything: exact numbers and symbols \
give exact results, complex numbers complex ones, and a SparseArray stays \
sparse in the steps that are products of matrices.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Einsum[\"ij,ij->\", {{1, 2}, {3, 4}}, {{1/2, I}, {x, Sqrt[2]}}]\n(* \
1/2 + 2 I + 3 x + 4 Sqrt[2] *)", "Input"], Cell["s = SparseArray[{{1, 1} -> \
1., {1000, 1000} -> 2.}, {1000, 1000}];\nEinsum[\"ij,jk->ik\", s, s] == s . \
s\n(* True *)", "Input"], Cell["8. A Kubo formula, as it is written", 
   "Section"], Cell["The optical conductivity of a two-band chain \
(Rice-Mele), sigma(w) = (i/N) Sum_k Sum_nm J_nm(k) J_mn(k) F_nm(k, w), with F \
= (f_n - f_m)/(E_m - E_n)/(w - E_m + E_n + i eta): k, n and m each appear in \
three tensors, and the einsum is the formula as it is written. First the \
bands, the current in the band basis and the occupations:", "Text"], 
  Cell["h[k_] := (1 + 0.6 Cos[k]) PauliMatrix[1] + 0.6 Sin[k] PauliMatrix[2] \
+ 0.4 PauliMatrix[3];\nks = Most[Subdivide[-Pi, Pi, 400]]; ws = \
Subdivide[0.5, 4., 350];\n{e, u} = Transpose[Eigensystem /@ (h /@ \
ks)];\ncurrent = MapThread[Conjugate[#1] . (D[h[q], q] /. q -> #2) . \
Transpose[#1] &, {u, ks}];\nf = UnitStep[-e];\nweight = Table[If[n == m, 0, \
(f[[q, n]] - f[[q, m]])/(e[[q, m]] - e[[q, n]])/(w - e[[q, m]] + e[[q, n]] + \
0.05 I)], {q, 400}, {n, 2}, {m, 2}, {w, ws}];\nDimensions /@ {current, \
weight}\n(* {{400, 2, 2}, {400, 2, 2, 351}}: 400 momenta, 2 bands, 351 \
frequencies *)", "Input"], Cell["sigma = I/400 Einsum[\"knm,kmn,knmw->w\", \
current, current, weight];\nListLinePlot[Transpose[{ws, Re[sigma]}], \
AxesLabel -> {\"\\[Omega]\", \"Re \\[Sigma]\"}]\n(* two peaks, near 1.2 and \
3.3: the edges of the interband transitions *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["Max[Abs[sigma[[{1, 100, 300}]] - Table[I/400 Sum[current[[q, n, m]] \
current[[q, m, n]] weight[[q, n, m, w]], {q, 400}, {n, 2}, {m, 2}], {w, {1, \
100, 300}}]]] < 10^-12\n(* True: the same as the sum written out *)", 
   "Input"], Cell["9. A tensor network, checked by hand", "Section"], 
  Cell["The norm of a random matrix-product state of four sites, by Einsum, \
against the same contraction written out with TensorContract and Dot, one \
step at a time.", "Text"], Cell["SeedRandom[5];\n{t1, t2, t3, t4} = \
{RandomReal[1, {2, 8}], RandomReal[1, {8, 2, 8}], RandomReal[1, {8, 2, 8}], \
RandomReal[1, {8, 2}]};\nnorm = Einsum[mps, t1, t2, t3, t4, t1, t2, t3, \
t4];\nstate = \
Flatten[TensorContract[TensorProduct[TensorContract[TensorProduct[TensorContr\
act[TensorProduct[t1, t2], {{2, 3}}], t3], {{3, 4}}], t4], {{4, \
5}}]];\nAbs[norm - state . state] < 10^-9 norm\n(* True: Einsum gives the \
squared norm of the state *)", "Input"], Cell["Where next", "Section"], 
  Cell["The wiki (github.com/n4sd4q/Summa/wiki) has the recipes: the syntax, \
repeated indices, orders and costs, speed, the diagrams. The showcase shows \
what the package can do, and the manual describes every function; ?Summa`* \
lists the functions and ?Name explains each one.", "Text"]}, Visible -> True, 
 WindowTitle -> "Summa \[Dash] guided tour", StyleDefinitions -> "Default.nb"]
